<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      THE ENTIRE PRIVACY CLAIM RESTS ON THIS FILE.

      App Sandbox is ON. That is what makes the absence of a network entitlement
      actually enforced by the OS rather than merely promised. A non-sandboxed app
      can open sockets regardless of what its entitlements say — sandboxing is what
      turns "we don't upload your data" into something a user can verify.

      DO NOT ADD:
        com.apple.security.network.client
        com.apple.security.network.server

      Adding either silently converts the strongest marketing claim this app has
      into a lie. If a future feature seems to need the network, it does not go in
      this target.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Access to exactly what the user drags in or picks. Nothing else.

      This is read-WRITE, not read-only, and the difference is not cosmetic:
      read-only would let the app scan a drive but silently fail to save a
      report, because the Save panel's grant is read-only too. The export is the
      whole point of the app, so it would have looked broken in the one moment
      that matters.

      The app still only ever touches what the user hands it, and it never
      writes to anything it scanned — only to the file chosen in a Save panel.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
